// `k = k` is not a leak. The right-hand side is a consuming position and takes
// the value before the write reaches the place, so by the time the place is
// written it holds nothing and there is nothing to lose.
//
// This is the same reason a write over a place a move emptied is allowed: the
// rule asks what the place holds, and a place that was taken from holds
// nothing. It is the position of the read that decides it, not the shape of
// the statement.
//
// The tag comes back as 7, so the value the write put back is the one that was
// there -- an accepted program that also proves nothing was destroyed on the
// way through.
fn main() -> UInt8 {
    var k = mint(7)
    k = k
    let t = tag(k)
    release(k)
    t
}
